    "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds **ADR-020: Page Caching for Back/Forward Navigation**. Makes Back/Forward instant by caching HTML per session, keyed by URL.\n\nThis is the largest deferred item from ADR-019 — Back currently re-fetches from the server, which is invisible on localhost but wrong on any real network.\n\nDocumentation only. Implementation (3 commits) lands in a follow-up PR after this ADR is reviewed and merged.\n\n## What the ADR specifies\n\n**`PageCache` class** (`src/browser/page_cache.hpp`) — header-only, no GTK, `unordered_map<string, string>`. Same design shape as `NavigationHistory`:\n\n```\nput(url, html)\nget(url) -> optional<string>\ncontains(url) / clear() / size() / bytes()\n```\n\n**Cache policy:**\n\n| NavKind | Cache read | Cache write |\n|---|---|---|\n| `New` | no | yes (on success) |\n| `Back` | **yes** (skip fetch on hit) | yes (on success, i.e. on miss) |\n| `Forward` | **yes** (skip fetch on hit) | yes (on success) |\n| `Reload` | no | yes (overwrites with fresh body) |\n\nNew and Reload always fetch fresh — without HTTP cache headers, serving stale content on an explicit navigation would be surprising. Both still populate the cache so a subsequent Back to the page is instant.\n\n## Key design points worth flagging\n\n- **Cache hit path skips fetch entirely.** No \"جارٍ الجلب...\" status, no button-disable. Back feels instant because it is.\n- **Reload refreshes cache.** Fetch fresh → `put` overwrites. So Back after Reload shows the new content.\n- **`record_visit` fires on cache hit too.** `RenderState::base_url` must update before render (relative-href resolution), and `visited_urls` re-insertion is idempotent.\n- **Elegant consequence: Back/Forward never cache-miss in v1.** ADR-019 pushes to history only on success; ADR-020 caches only on success. Therefore every history entry is in the cache. ADR-019's rollback-on-failure becomes unreachable — kept for safety against future eviction.\n- **No eviction.** Unbounded in v1. `bytes()` exists for future diagnostics, not enforced as a cap.\n\n## Deferred\n\n- Eviction / LRU / size cap (interacts with the \"Back always hits\" invariant — its own ADR)\n- Persistence across sessions\n- HTTP cache semantics (`Cache-Control`, `ETag`, `Vary`)\n- Conditional GETs (`If-Modified-Since`, `If-None-Match`)\n- Bfcache-style page state (form values, JS state, scroll)\n- Cache invalidation on POST (no POST in v1)\n\n## The definitive test (visual verification)\n\nKill the HTTP server mid-session and click Back. If the browser still navigates — the cache is being used. Then click Reload with the server down — should fail (proving Reload bypasses cache). This is the manual proof the unit tests can't provide.\n\n## Reviewer checklist\n\n- [ ] `PageCache` API sensible? (`get`/`put`/`contains`/`size`/`bytes` — anything missing?)\n- [ ] Cache policy table correct? (New writes but doesn't read; Reload neither reads nor is a no-op)\n- [ ] Deferred items in the right place?\n- [ ] Is \"no eviction\" acceptable for v1? (I think yes — local browser, dozens of pages max)\n\n## Test plan\n\nNo code touched, so no test changes. Will run `cmake --build build && ./build/tests/honeybee_tests` after merge to confirm `main` still clean (245 cases, 960 assertions, unchanged).\n",
